-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/6911d087-1a16-4909-b6af-ff9b5bc498d9
-- title:
--   Erschler–Zheng §§1–3, 5, 8 — word length, growth, random walks, entropy, the Poisson boundary, the induced kernel and cube independence
-- statement:
--   Definitions from Erschler and Zheng, *Growth of periodic Grigorchuk groups*, Invent. Math. 219 (2020) 1069–1155, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-019-00922-0; page numbers are those of arXiv:1802.09077v2. Word length and growth (p. 1), harmonic functions, entropy and the Poisson boundary (pp. 2, 9–10), the volume exponent (p. 58), the induced transition kernel on an orbit (p. 18) and the cube independence property (p. 26). Balls are the published `Chou.wordBall S n` (Chou bundle): the products of at most $n$ factors, each in $S$ or with its inverse in $S$.
--
--   A measure on a group $G$ is a function $\mu : G \to \mathbb R$, and every sum over the group is an unconditional sum (`tsum`), which takes the value $0$ on a family that is not summable. Actions are right actions, written `x <• g` for a `MulAction Hᵐᵒᵖ X`, so that $x \cdot (gh) = (x \cdot g) \cdot h$, following p. 9: “We consider right group actions $\Gamma \curvearrowright X$ and write the action of group element $g$ on $x \in X$ as $x \cdot g$.”
--
--   - `wordLength S g` and `growth S r` are p. 1: “Let $\Gamma$ be a finitely generated group and $S$ be a finite generating set of $\Gamma$. The word length $l_S(\gamma)$ of an element $\gamma \in \Gamma$ is the smallest integer $n \geqslant 0$ for which there exists $s_1, \ldots, s_n \in S \cup S^{-1}$ such that $\gamma = s_1 \ldots s_n$. The growth function $v_{\Gamma,S}(n)$ counts the number of elements with word length $l_S(\gamma) \leqslant n$, that is $v_{\Gamma,S}(n) = \#\{\gamma \in \Gamma : l_S(\gamma) \leqslant n\}$.” The definitions take any set $S$, not only a finite generating set: `wordLength S g` is the least $n$ with $g$ in `Chou.wordBall S n`, and it is $0$ for an element outside the subgroup generated by $S$, where $l_S$ is undefined; every statement that uses them assumes $S$ finite and generating, as the sentence does. `growth S r` is the number of elements of `Chou.wordBall S ⌊r⌋₊`: the growth function at a real radius, since the paper evaluates $v_{\Gamma,S}$ at radii such as $L^\omega_n$ and $R_n = n\phi(\varrho_n)$; $\lfloor r \rfloor_+$ is $0$ for $r < 0$. An infinite ball would count as $0$; every statement that uses `growth` assumes $S$ finite.
--   - `ball S R` is $\{g : l_S(g) \le R\}$ for a real $R$: the ball $B(id, R)$ of Corollary 8.2 and Theorem 8.3 (pp. 57–58), written $B(e, R)$ on p. 10, which the paper uses without a defining sentence. Through the junk value of `wordLength`, it contains every element outside the subgroup generated by $S$ once $R \ge 0$; every statement that uses it has $S$ generating the group.
--   - `HasVolumeExponent S α` is p. 58: “We say the volume exponent of $G$ exists if the limit $\lim_{r \to \infty} \log\log v_{G,S}(r)/\log r$ exists and the limit value, if exists, is called the volume exponent of $G$.” `HasVolumeExponent S α` says that $\log\log v_{G,S}(n)/\log n$ tends to $\alpha$ along the integers $n$. This is the same as the limit over real $r$ (`ErschlerZheng.hasVolumeExponent_iff_tendsto_log_log_growth_div_log`).
--   - `IsProbability μ`, `IsSymmetric μ` and `mass μ A`: $\mu \ge 0$ with total mass $1$; $\mu(g^{-1}) = \mu(g)$ for every $g$; and $\mu(A) = \sum_{g \in A} \mu(g)$.
--   - `IsNondegenerate μ` is p. 9: “Given a probability measure $\mu$ on $\Gamma$, we say $\mu$ is non-degenerate if its support generates $G$ as a semigroup.” (Here $G$ stands for $\Gamma$.) It says that the subsemigroup generated by $\{g : \mu(g) \ne 0\}$ is the whole group.
--   - `uniformMeasure F` is p. 9: “Given a finite set $F$, $\mathbf u_F$ denotes the uniform distribution on $F$.” It gives mass $1/\#F$ to each point of $F$; for an infinite $F$ it is the zero function.
--   - `conv μ ν` and `convPow μ n`: $(\mu \star \nu)(g) = \sum_h \mu(h)\,\nu(h^{-1}g)$, with $\mu^{(0)}$ the point mass at the identity and $\mu^{(n+1)} = \mu^{(n)} \star \mu$. The paper writes $\mu^{(n)}$ without defining it; it is read as the $n$-th convolution power. The $\mu$-random walk itself is defined on p. 9: “Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be i.i.d. random variables with distribution $\mu$ on $\Gamma$ and $W_n = X_1 \ldots X_n$.”
--   - `entropy μ` and `HasFiniteEntropy μ` are p. 10: “The (Shannon) entropy of a probability measure $\mu$ on $\Gamma$ is $H(\mu) = -\sum_{g \in \Gamma} \mu(g) \log \mu(g)$.” `entropy μ` is $\sum_g -\mu(g)\log\mu(g)$ with $0 \log 0 = 0$; it is $0$, not $+\infty$, when the series diverges, so finite entropy is the separate predicate `HasFiniteEntropy μ`, the summability of that series.
--   - `asymptoticEntropy μ` is p. 10: “In the case that the measure $\mu$ has finite entropy $H(\mu) < \infty$, the (Avez) asymptotic entropy of $\mu$ is defined as $\mathbf h_\mu = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{H(\mu^{(n)})}{n}$.” It is the limit of $H(\mu^{(n)})/n$ when that limit exists, and an unspecified real number otherwise. That the limit exists for a probability of finite entropy on a countable group is part of the milestone `ErschlerZheng.hasFiniteEntropy_convPow_and_tendsto_entropy_convPow_div`.
--   - `IsHarmonic μ f` is p. 2: “Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on a group $\Gamma$. A function $f : \Gamma \to \mathbb R$ is $\mu$-harmonic if $f(x) = \sum_{y \in G} f(xy)\mu(y)$ for all $x \in \Gamma$.” (The sum is over $\Gamma$.)
--   - `HasNontrivialPoissonBoundary μ` is p. 2: “Among several definitions of the Poisson boundary of $(\Gamma, \mu)$, also called the Poisson-Furstenberg boundary, one is formulated in terms of the measure theoretic boundary that represents all bounded $\mu$-harmonic functions on $\Gamma$, see [36]. In particular the Poisson boundary of $(\Gamma, \mu)$ is trivial if and only if all bounded $\mu$-harmonic functions on $\Gamma$ are constant.” The boundary itself is never used, only whether it is trivial, so the definition is through harmonic functions: there is a bounded $\mu$-harmonic $f : G \to \mathbb R$ that is not constant on `Submonoid.closure (Function.support μ)`, the set of products of elements of the support of $\mu$ (the identity included). For a non-degenerate $\mu$ the definition is exactly the quoted criterion: some bounded $\mu$-harmonic function on $G$ is not constant (`ErschlerZheng.hasNontrivialPoissonBoundary_iff_exists_bounded_isHarmonic_ne_of_isNondegenerate`). Read literally for every $\mu$, the quoted criterion would give the point mass $\delta_{id}$ on a non-trivial group a non-trivial boundary, against the entropy criterion of p. 10; the definition used here gives it a trivial one (`ErschlerZheng.isProbability_and_hasFiniteEntropy_and_asymptoticEntropy_eq_zero_and_forall_isHarmonic_and_exists_bounded_isHarmonic_ne_and_not_hasNontrivialPoissonBoundary_indicator_singleton_one`). A trivial Poisson boundary is written `¬ HasNontrivialPoissonBoundary μ`.
--   - `stepMeasure μ`, `pathMeasure μ x` and `IsShiftInvariant A` are p. 9: “Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on a countable group $\Gamma$. For $x \in \Gamma$, let $\mathbb P_x$ be the law of the random walk trajectory $(W_0, W_1, \ldots)$ starting at $W_0 = x$. Endow the space of infinite trajectories $G^{\mathbb N}$ with the usual Borel $\sigma$-field $\mathcal A$. The shift $\mathfrak s : \Gamma^{\mathbb N} \to \Gamma^{\mathbb N}$ acts by $\mathfrak s(g_1, g_2, \ldots) = (g_2, g_3, \ldots)$. The *invariant $\sigma$-field* $\mathcal I$ is defined as $\mathcal I = \{A \in \mathcal A : \mathfrak s^{-1}(A) = A\}$, it is also referred to as the *stationary $\sigma$-field*.” `stepMeasure μ` is $\sum_g \mu(g)\,\delta_g$; `pathMeasure μ x` is the image of the product of countably many copies of it under $\xi \mapsto (n \mapsto x\,\xi_0 \cdots \xi_{n-1})$, the law of the trajectory $W_n = x X_1 \cdots X_n$ of the walk started at $x$, on $\Gamma^{\mathbb N}$ with the product $\sigma$-algebra. For a probability $\mu$ on a group whose singletons are measurable, as under the discrete $\sigma$-algebra, `pathMeasure μ x` is a probability measure (`ErschlerZheng.isProbabilityMeasure_pathMeasure`). `IsShiftInvariant A` is the set equality $\mathfrak s^{-1}(A) = A$.
--   - `rightOrbit K x` is the orbit $x \cdot K = \{x \cdot k : k \in K\}$ of a subgroup $K$, as in p. 17 (“the orbits $x \cdot L = x \cdot G$”).
--   - `orbitKernel K μ o` is p. 18: “Given a probability measure $\mu$ on $G$, its induced transition kernel $P_\mu$ on an orbit $o \cdot G$ is given by $P_\mu(x, y) = \sum_{g \in G} \mu(g) \mathbf 1_{\{y = x \cdot g\}}$.” Here $\mu$ is a function on the subgroup $K$ and the kernel is a function on pairs of points of the orbit $o \cdot K$.
--   - `cubeProd g n ε`, `IsCubeIndependent Y k g` and `quasiCubicSet g k n` are p. 26: “For a sequence of elements $g_1, g_2, \ldots \in G$, we say the sequence satisfies *the cube independence property* on $x_0 \cdot G$ with parameters $\mathbf k = (k_n)_{n=1}^\infty$, $k_n \in \mathbb N$, if for any $n \geqslant 1$ and $x \in x_0 \cdot G$, the map $\{0, 1, \ldots, k_1\} \times \ldots \times \{0, 1, \ldots, k_n\} \to x_0 \cdot G$, $(\varepsilon_1, \ldots \varepsilon_n) \mapsto x \cdot g_n^{\varepsilon_n} \ldots g_1^{\varepsilon_1}$ is injective. When the parameters $k_n$ are constant 1, we omit reference to $\mathbf k$ and say the sequence has the cube independence property.” `cubeProd g n ε` is $g_n^{\varepsilon_n} \cdots g_1^{\varepsilon_1}$, and `IsCubeIndependent Y k g` is the property on a set $Y$ of points (the orbit $x_0 \cdot G$ in every use): for $n \ge 1$ and $x \in Y$, exponent sequences with $\varepsilon_j \le k_j$ for $1 \le j \le n$ that give the same point $x \cdot g_n^{\varepsilon_n} \cdots g_1^{\varepsilon_1}$ agree at $1, \ldots, n$. Sequences are indexed from $1$; `g 0` is never used, and the exponents are functions $\mathbb N \to \mathbb N$ of which only the values at $1, \ldots, n$ matter. `quasiCubicSet g k n` is the set $F_n = \{g_n^{\varepsilon_n} \ldots g_1^{\varepsilon_1} : \varepsilon_j \in \{0, 1, \ldots, k_j\}, 1 \leqslant j \leqslant n\}$ of Proposition 5.4 (p. 26), which Remark 5.5 calls “an $n$-quasi-cubic set”.
-- source:
--   Erschler, A. and Zheng, T., Growth of periodic Grigorchuk groups, Invent. Math. 219 (2020) 1069–1155, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-019-00922-0 (arXiv:1802.09077v2, whose page numbers are used), pp. 1–2, 9–11, §1–§2 (word length, growth, measures, entropy, harmonic functions, Poisson boundary)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Chou_Growth

/-!
# Word length, growth, random walks and cube independence (Erschler–Zheng §1, §2.1, §3, §5)

A. Erschler and T. Zheng, *Growth of periodic Grigorchuk groups*, arXiv:1802.09077v2; page
numbers are those of the arXiv version.

Conventions used in this file:
- Balls are `Chou.wordBall S n`: products of at most `n` factors, each in `S` or with its inverse
  in `S`.
- A measure on a group `G` is a function `μ : G → ℝ`; `IsProbability μ` says it is nonnegative with
  total mass 1. Every sum over the group is a `tsum`.
- Right actions: with `open scoped RightActions`, `x <• g` is `MulOpposite.op g • x` for an action
  `MulAction Hᵐᵒᵖ X`, so that `x <• (g * h) = (x <• g) <• h`.
- Sequences `g 1, g 2, …` are indexed from 1; the value `g 0` is not used.
- `wordLength S g` is `0` (the value of `sInf ∅`) when `g` is no product of elements of `S ∪ S⁻¹`;
  `asymptoticEntropy` is a `Filter.limUnder`, unspecified when the sequence does not converge.
-/

namespace ErschlerZheng

open scoped RightActions

variable {G : Type*} [Group G]

/-- p. 1: the word length `l_S(g)`, the least `n` such that `g` is a product of `n` elements of
`S ∪ S⁻¹` (`0` if `g` is no such product). -/
noncomputable def wordLength (S : Set G) (g : G) : ℕ := sInf {n | g ∈ Chou.wordBall S n}

/-- p. 1: the growth function `v_{G,S}(r)` at a real radius `r`: the number of elements of
`Chou.wordBall S ⌊r⌋₊`. -/
noncomputable def growth (S : Set G) (r : ℝ) : ℕ := Nat.card (Chou.wordBall S ⌊r⌋₊)

/-- p. 57: the ball `B(id, R) = {g : l_S(g) ⩽ R}`. -/
def ball (S : Set G) (R : ℝ) : Set G := {g | (wordLength S g : ℝ) ≤ R}

/-- pp. 1, 58: `log log v_{G,S}(n) / log n` tends to `α` as `n → ∞`. -/
def HasVolumeExponent (S : Set G) (α : ℝ) : Prop :=
  Filter.Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => Real.log (Real.log (growth S n)) / Real.log n) Filter.atTop
    (nhds α)

/-- A probability measure on `G`: `μ ⩾ 0` with total mass 1. -/
def IsProbability (μ : G → ℝ) : Prop := (∀ g, 0 ≤ μ g) ∧ HasSum μ 1

/-- A symmetric measure: `μ(g⁻¹) = μ(g)` for every `g`. -/
def IsSymmetric (μ : G → ℝ) : Prop := ∀ g, μ g⁻¹ = μ g

/-- p. 9: the support of `μ` generates `G` as a semigroup. -/
def IsNondegenerate (μ : G → ℝ) : Prop := Subsemigroup.closure {g | μ g ≠ 0} = ⊤

/-- The mass `μ(A) = Σ_{g ∈ A} μ(g)` of a set `A`. -/
noncomputable def mass (μ : G → ℝ) (A : Set G) : ℝ := ∑' g, A.indicator μ g

open scoped Classical in
/-- p. 9: the uniform distribution `u_F`, mass `1/#F` at each point of `F` (for infinite `F`,
`Nat.card F = 0` and this is the zero function). -/
noncomputable def uniformMeasure (F : Set G) : G → ℝ :=
  fun g => if g ∈ F then (Nat.card F : ℝ)⁻¹ else 0

/-- The convolution `(μ ⋆ ν)(g) = Σ_h μ(h) ν(h⁻¹g)`. -/
noncomputable def conv (μ ν : G → ℝ) : G → ℝ := fun g => ∑' h, μ h * ν (h⁻¹ * g)

open scoped Classical in
/-- The convolution powers `μ^{(n)}`: `μ^{(0)}` is the point mass at the identity and
`μ^{(n+1)} = μ^{(n)} ⋆ μ`. -/
noncomputable def convPow (μ : G → ℝ) : ℕ → G → ℝ
  | 0 => fun g => if g = 1 then 1 else 0
  | n + 1 => conv (convPow μ n) μ

/-- pp. 2, 10: the Shannon entropy `H(μ) = −Σ_g μ(g) log μ(g)`. -/
noncomputable def entropy (μ : G → ℝ) : ℝ := ∑' g, Real.negMulLog (μ g)

/-- p. 2: finite entropy: the series `Σ_g −μ(g) log μ(g)` is summable. -/
def HasFiniteEntropy (μ : G → ℝ) : Prop := Summable fun g => Real.negMulLog (μ g)

/-- p. 10: the asymptotic entropy `h_μ = lim_{n→∞} H(μ^{(n)})/n`, taken with `Filter.limUnder`. -/
noncomputable def asymptoticEntropy (μ : G → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  Filter.limUnder Filter.atTop fun n : ℕ => entropy (convPow μ n) / n

/-- p. 2: `f` is `μ`-harmonic, `f(x) = Σ_y f(xy) μ(y)` for all `x`. -/
def IsHarmonic (μ : G → ℝ) (f : G → ℝ) : Prop := ∀ x, f x = ∑' y, f (x * y) * μ y

/-- pp. 2, 10: the Poisson boundary of `(G, μ)` is non-trivial: there is a bounded `μ`-harmonic
function on `G` that is not constant on `Submonoid.closure (Function.support μ)`, the products of
elements of the support of `μ` (the identity included). -/
def HasNontrivialPoissonBoundary (μ : G → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ f : G → ℝ, (∃ C, ∀ x, |f x| ≤ C) ∧ IsHarmonic μ f ∧
    ∃ x ∈ Submonoid.closure (Function.support μ), ∃ y ∈ Submonoid.closure (Function.support μ),
      f x ≠ f y

/-- p. 9: the step distribution `μ` as a measure, `Σ_g μ(g) δ_g`. -/
noncomputable def stepMeasure [MeasurableSpace G] (μ : G → ℝ) : MeasureTheory.Measure G :=
  MeasureTheory.Measure.sum fun g => ENNReal.ofReal (μ g) • MeasureTheory.Measure.dirac g

/-- p. 9: `ℙ_x`, the law of the trajectory `(W_0, W_1, …)`, `W_n = x X_1 ⋯ X_n`: the image of
the product of copies of `stepMeasure μ` under `ξ ↦ (n ↦ x ξ_0 ⋯ ξ_{n-1})`. -/
noncomputable def pathMeasure [MeasurableSpace G] (μ : G → ℝ) (x : G) :
    MeasureTheory.Measure (ℕ → G) :=
  (MeasureTheory.Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => stepMeasure μ).map
    fun ξ n => x * (List.ofFn fun i : Fin n => ξ i).prod

/-- p. 9: `𝔰⁻¹(A) = A` for the shift `𝔰` of trajectories. -/
def IsShiftInvariant (A : Set (ℕ → G)) : Prop :=
  (fun w : ℕ → G => fun n => w (n + 1)) ⁻¹' A = A

section Actions

variable {H X : Type*} [Group H] [MulAction Hᵐᵒᵖ X]

/-- p. 17: the orbit `x·K = {x·k : k ∈ K}` of `x` under a subgroup `K`, for a right action. -/
def rightOrbit (K : Subgroup H) (x : X) : Set X := {y | ∃ k ∈ K, y = x <• k}

open scoped Classical in
/-- p. 18: the induced transition kernel `P_μ(x, y) = Σ_{g ∈ K} μ(g) 1{y = x·g}` on the orbit
`o·K`, for `μ` on `K`. -/
noncomputable def orbitKernel (K : Subgroup H) (μ : K → ℝ) (o : X) :
    rightOrbit K o → rightOrbit K o → ℝ :=
  fun x y => ∑' g : K, if (x : X) <• (g : H) = y then μ g else 0

/-- p. 26: the product `g_n^{ε_n} ⋯ g_1^{ε_1}` for a sequence `g 1, g 2, …` (the value `g 0` is
not used) and exponents `ε`. -/
def cubeProd (g : ℕ → H) (n : ℕ) (ε : ℕ → ℕ) : H :=
  ((List.range n).reverse.map fun i => g (i + 1) ^ ε (i + 1)).prod

/-- pp. 5, 26: the cube independence property on `Y` with parameters `k`: for every `n ⩾ 1` and
`x ∈ Y`, the map `ε ↦ x·g_n^{ε_n} ⋯ g_1^{ε_1}` is injective on
`{0, …, k_1} × ⋯ × {0, …, k_n}`. -/
def IsCubeIndependent (Y : Set X) (k : ℕ → ℕ) (g : ℕ → H) : Prop :=
  ∀ n ≥ 1, ∀ x ∈ Y, ∀ ε ε' : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ j, 1 ≤ j → j ≤ n → ε j ≤ k j ∧ ε' j ≤ k j) →
    x <• cubeProd g n ε = x <• cubeProd g n ε' → ∀ j, 1 ≤ j → j ≤ n → ε j = ε' j

/-- p. 26: the `n`-quasi-cubic set `F_n = {g_n^{ε_n} ⋯ g_1^{ε_1} : ε_j ∈ {0, …, k_j}}`. -/
def quasiCubicSet (g : ℕ → H) (k : ℕ → ℕ) (n : ℕ) : Set H :=
  {h | ∃ ε : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ j, 1 ≤ j → j ≤ n → ε j ≤ k j) ∧ h = cubeProd g n ε}

end Actions

end ErschlerZheng


